  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so overshooting shows there first. A learning rate above 0.5 makes the x swings grow (|1 - 4·lr| > 1). Runs start at x = 0.9, z = 1.7.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with mean zero and standard deviation 1.5·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B, like sampling B of 64 points. It is exactly zero at B = 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at their true step length in the bowl's units (scale 1). Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes. Grey is the full-data step, −learning rate × true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes past 10 (diverged). Noise comes from a fixed seed: Reset replays the same sequence, and a new run takes the next values.",
    "A step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches a single step can raise it. Positions beyond the bowl are capped on screen, but the Loss readout keeps the true value."
  ]
}